body:
Ok I will explain this, when using set_visibility_public after peer disconnect to remove the peer id from a MultiplayerSynchronizer, what happens is the following:

```cpp
void MultiplayerSynchronizer::set_visibility_public(bool p_visible) {
	set_visibility_for(0, p_visible);
}
```

This will call [set_visibility_for]:
Here we manage to remove the peerId from peer_visibility correctly!

```cpp
void MultiplayerSynchronizer::set_visibility_for(int p_peer, bool p_visible) {
	if (peer_visibility.has(p_peer) == p_visible) {
		return;
	}
	if (p_visible) {
		peer_visibility.insert(p_peer);
	} else {
		peer_visibility.erase(p_peer);
	}
	update_visibility(p_peer);
}
```


This will call [update_visibility]

```cpp
void MultiplayerSynchronizer::update_visibility(int p_for_peer) {
#ifdef TOOLS_ENABLED
	if (Engine::get_singleton()->is_editor_hint()) {
		return;
	}
#endif
	Node *node = is_inside_tree() ? get_node_or_null(root_path) : nullptr;
	if (node && get_multiplayer()->has_multiplayer_peer() && is_multiplayer_authority()) {
		emit_signal(SceneStringName(visibility_changed), p_for_peer);
	}
}
```



This will emit [visibility_changed] and call:

```cpp
void SceneReplicationInterface::_visibility_changed(int p_peer, ObjectID p_sid) {
	MultiplayerSynchronizer *sync = get_id_as<MultiplayerSynchronizer>(p_sid);
	ERR_FAIL_NULL(sync); // Bug.
	Node *node = sync->get_root_node();
	ERR_FAIL_NULL(node); // Bug.
	const ObjectID oid = node->get_instance_id();
	if (spawned_nodes.has(oid) && p_peer != multiplayer->get_unique_id()) {
		_update_spawn_visibility(p_peer, oid);
	}
	_update_sync_visibility(p_peer, sync);
}
```



That will finally call [_update_sync_visibility] or [_update_spawn_visibility] (any will error out):


```cpp
Error SceneReplicationInterface::_update_sync_visibility(int p_peer, MultiplayerSynchronizer *p_sync) {
	ERR_FAIL_NULL_V(p_sync, ERR_BUG);
	if (!_has_authority(p_sync) || p_peer == multiplayer->get_unique_id()) {
		return OK;
	}

	const ObjectID &sid = p_sync->get_instance_id();
	bool is_visible = p_sync->is_visible_to(p_peer);
	if (p_peer == 0) {
		for (KeyValue<int, PeerInfo> &E : peers_info) {
			// Might be visible to this specific peer.
			bool is_visible_to_peer = is_visible || p_sync->is_visible_to(E.key);
			if (is_visible_to_peer == E.value.sync_nodes.has(sid)) {
				continue;
			}
			if (is_visible_to_peer) {
				E.value.sync_nodes.insert(sid);
			} else {
				E.value.sync_nodes.erase(sid);
				E.value.last_watch_usecs.erase(sid);
			}
		}
		return OK;
	} else {
		ERR_FAIL_COND_V(!peers_info.has(p_peer), ERR_INVALID_PARAMETER);
		if (is_visible == peers_info[p_peer].sync_nodes.has(sid)) {
			return OK;
		}
		if (is_visible) {
			peers_info[p_peer].sync_nodes.insert(sid);
		} else {
			peers_info[p_peer].sync_nodes.erase(sid);
			peers_info[p_peer].last_watch_usecs.erase(sid);
		}
		return OK;
	}
}
```

Now what happens is that the peers_info no longer holds the peerId because it was disconnected, however I still need to clean the MultiplayerSynchronizer peer_visibility to remove the peerId disconnected. This leads to an error because of this line:

		ERR_FAIL_COND_V(!peers_info.has(p_peer), ERR_INVALID_PARAMETER);

You can also check that nothing is done to remove the peerId from peer_visibility when disconnected

```cpp
void SceneReplicationInterface::on_peer_change(int p_id, bool p_connected) {
	if (p_connected) {
		peers_info[p_id] = PeerInfo();
		for (const ObjectID &oid : spawned_nodes) {
			_update_spawn_visibility(p_id, oid);
		}
		for (const ObjectID &oid : sync_nodes) {
			_update_sync_visibility(p_id, get_id_as<MultiplayerSynchronizer>(oid));
		}
	} else {
		ERR_FAIL_COND(!peers_info.has(p_id));
		_free_remotes(peers_info[p_id]);
		peers_info.erase(p_id);
	}
}
```

This is both a bug and a small memory leak. If the only way to clean `peer_visibility` is by using `set_visibility_for`, and that method throws an error, then it is a bug. If you do not use `set_visibility_for` , `peer_visibility` is left in an inconsistent state.

For example, if you call `is_visible_to` and pass a disconnected peer, it will still return `TRUE`, even though the peer has been disconnected, because `peer_visibility` was never cleaned up.


What happens if another peer get the same Id from a previous peer? Than the object that was supposed to be not visibile for that new peer will be visible.



My suggestion to fix this issue is that when a peer is disconnected clean it from all the synchronizers that this peer is current visible.



```cpp
void SceneReplicationInterface::on_peer_change(int p_id, bool p_connected) {
	if (p_connected) {
		peers_info[p_id] = PeerInfo();
		for (const ObjectID &oid : spawned_nodes) {
			_update_spawn_visibility(p_id, oid);
		}
		for (const ObjectID &oid : sync_nodes) {
			_update_sync_visibility(p_id, get_id_as<MultiplayerSynchronizer>(oid));
		}
	} else {
		ERR_FAIL_COND(!peers_info.has(p_id));
		_free_remotes(peers_info[p_id]);
               // SNIPPET
		for (const ObjectID &oid : sync_nodes) {
			MultiplayerSynchronizer* sync = get_id_as<MultiplayerSynchronizer>(oid);
                        // REMOVE HERE IF VISIBLE
		}

		peers_info.erase(p_id);
	}
}
```
